+    <section class="journal" aria-labelledby="journal-title">
+      <div class="journal-heading">
+        <div>
+          <p class="journal-eyebrow">From origin to your cup</p>
+          <h2 id="journal-title">Roaster's Journal</h2>
+        </div>
+        <button type="button" id="summarize-button" class="btn" aria-controls="journal-summary"
+          aria-describedby="journal-status" disabled>Summarize</button>
+      </div>
+
+      <article id="journal-text" class="journal-copy" aria-labelledby="sourcing-title">
+        <h3 id="sourcing-title">Good coffee begins long before the roast</h3>
+        <p>Every bag of coffee has a story that begins with soil, weather, and the people who tend the trees.
+          Our sourcing process starts with a simple question: what makes this harvest worth sharing? Before
+          we think about a roast profile, we look for beans with a clear sense of place and for producers who
+          care about how their coffee is grown. Altitude, shade, rainfall, and the variety of the coffee plant
+          all shape what eventually reaches your cup. Understanding those details helps us choose coffee
+          for its character, rather than chase a flavor that tastes the same everywhere.</p>
+
+        <p>We seek out growers, cooperatives, and sourcing partners who can tell us where a lot comes from
+          and how it was handled. Traceability means more than a country printed on a label. We want to
+          understand the farm or community behind a harvest, when the cherries were picked, and how the
+          beans were processed. We also ask about working conditions, payment, and the challenges growers
+          face from season to season. Responsible sourcing is an ongoing conversation, and we value partners
+          who are willing to share both their progress and the work still ahead.</p>
+
+        <p>Once samples arrive, we roast small batches and taste them side by side in a process called
+          cupping. We pay attention to aroma, sweetness, acidity, body, and the finish left after each sip.
+          A bright, fruit-forward coffee might shine as a seasonal filter offering, while a rounder lot
+          with cocoa notes could become the foundation of an espresso blend. We taste more than once,
+          because a single impressive cup is not enough. The coffee needs to hold its quality across
+          samples and work well in the ways our customers actually brew it.</p>
+
+        <p>Processing is another important part of the story. Washed coffees often offer clarity and
+          a crisp expression of their origin; naturally processed coffees can bring fuller fruit flavors
+          and a softer sweetness. Neither method is automatically better. What matters is careful cherry
+          selection, thoughtful fermentation, and even drying. We consider how water is used, how the farm
+          manages its soil, and whether growing practices support the land over time. Those questions sit
+          alongside flavor when we decide which coffees to bring into the roastery.</p>
+
+        <p>After selecting a lot, we work with our partners on transport and storage so that the green
+          beans arrive in good condition. At the roastery, we develop a profile that brings out the coffee's
+          strengths, making small adjustments to heat and time rather than treating every origin alike.
+          We cup production batches and keep notes as the coffee rests and develops. Freshness matters,
+          but so does giving each roast the attention it deserves. Our aim is a balanced cup that lets
+          the grower's work remain part of the experience.</p>
+
+        <p>Sourcing does not end when a shipment arrives. Harvests change, relationships deepen, and
+          there is always more to learn. We use what we taste and hear throughout the season to guide
+          our next choices, favoring lasting relationships over one-off discoveries. When you enjoy a
+          cup at The Daily Grind, we want you to feel connected to that whole journey: thoughtful growing,
+          careful selection, and roasting that respects the bean. Good coffee is the result of many
+          hands, and our job is to carry that care all the way to your cup.</p>
+      </article>
+
